*(
    title: Vectors in Three Dimensions
    description: Adding a third component, what carries over unchanged from two dimensions, and the one thing that does not.
    tags: vectors, three dimensions, magnitude, unit vector, components, extension 2
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    A vector in the plane is two numbers: how far across, and how far up. The vector $3\mathbf{i} + 2\mathbf{j}$ on the canvas has magnitude $\sqrt{13}$, by Pythagoras on the two components.

    ===

    A vector in space is three numbers. Add a third basis vector $\mathbf{k}$, pointing out of the page at right angles to both $\mathbf{i}$ and $\mathbf{j}$, and $3\mathbf{i} + 2\mathbf{j} + 6\mathbf{k}$ is a vector that goes three across, two up and six out. Its magnitude is $7$, by the same rule with one more square under the root.

    definition{
        A vector written as $a_1\mathbf{i} + a_2\mathbf{j} + a_3\mathbf{k}$, or as the ordered triple $(a_1, a_2, a_3)$, or as a column with the three components stacked. The three forms name the same vector, and $\mathbf{i}$, $\mathbf{j}$, $\mathbf{k}$ are the unit vectors along the three axes.
    }(
        term: Component form in three dimensions
    )

    ===

    The rule is Pythagoras used twice. The first two components give a length $\sqrt{13}$ along the floor; that length and the third component form a second right angle, and $13 + 36 = 49$. Nothing in the argument depends on there being only three components, which is why the formula looks the way it does.

    Almost everything Extension 1 established about vectors in the plane survives the move to space in the same way: addition is still tip to tail, scalar multiplication still stretches without turning, and every formula gains one more term.
}[
    #### In the plane

    equation{
        \mathbf{a} = 3\mathbf{i} + 2\mathbf{j}, \qquad |\mathbf{a}| = \sqrt{3^2 + 2^2} = \sqrt{13}
    }

    cue{
        #### In space

        equation{
            \mathbf{a} = 3\mathbf{i} + 2\mathbf{j} + 6\mathbf{k}, \qquad |\mathbf{a}| = \sqrt{3^2 + 2^2 + 6^2} = \sqrt{49} = 7
        }
    }(
        in: 1
    )

    cue{
        #### Pythagoras twice

        equation{
            \left(\sqrt{3^2 + 2^2}\right)^2 + 6^2 = 13 + 36 = 49
        }
    }(
        in: 2
    )
]

scene{
    # Magnitude, distance and unit vectors

    A point in space has a position vector, the arrow from the origin to it, and the vector from one point to another is the difference of their position vectors: $\overrightarrow{PQ} = \overrightarrow{OQ} - \overrightarrow{OP}$. For $P(1, 2, -1)$ and $Q(4, 0, 5)$ that is $(3, -2, 6)$.

    ===

    The distance between the points is the magnitude of that vector, $\sqrt{9 + 4 + 36} = 7$. There is no separate distance formula to learn: it is the magnitude formula applied to a difference.

    ===

    Dividing a vector by its own magnitude leaves a vector of length $1$ in the same direction, which is the way a direction is named without a length attached.

    definition{
        A vector of magnitude $1$. The unit vector in the direction of $\mathbf{a}$ is $\hat{\mathbf{a}} = \dfrac{\mathbf{a}}{|\mathbf{a}|}$, and any vector is its magnitude times its unit vector.
    }(
        term: Unit vector
    )

    So a vector of length $14$ in the direction of $\overrightarrow{PQ}$ is $14 \cdot \frac{1}{7}(3, -2, 6) = (6, -4, 12)$. Separating a vector into a length and a direction this way is the habit that makes the rest of the area go smoothly.
}[
    #### The vector between two points

    equation{
        \overrightarrow{PQ} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 0 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -2 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}
    }

    cue{
        #### Its magnitude is the distance from $P$ to $Q$

        equation{
            \left|\overrightarrow{PQ}\right| = \sqrt{3^2 + (-2)^2 + 6^2} = 7
        }
    }(
        in: 1
    )

    cue{
        #### The unit vector in that direction

        equation{
            \hat{\mathbf{a}} = \frac{1}{7}\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -2 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}
        }
    }(
        in: 2
    )
]

scene{
    # Addition and scalar multiplication

    Both operations work one component at a time. Adding two vectors adds their first components, their second components and their third; multiplying by a scalar multiplies each component by it. The table works both out for $\mathbf{a} = (2, -1, 3)$ and $\mathbf{b} = (1, 4, -2)$.

    Scalar multiplication is what makes *parallel* a computable word. Multiplying by a positive number stretches a vector without turning it, and by a negative number reverses it, so two vectors point along the same line exactly when one is a multiple of the other.

    definition{
        Vectors $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ are parallel when $\mathbf{b} = \lambda\mathbf{a}$ for some non-zero scalar $\lambda$. A negative $\lambda$ means they point in opposite directions along the same line.
    }(
        term: Parallel vectors
    )

    Checking is a matter of ratios: $(-4, 2, -6)$ is $-2\mathbf{a}$, so it is parallel to $\mathbf{a}$, while $(-4, 2, 6)$ is not, because no single scalar produces all three components.

    Three points are collinear when the vectors between them are parallel — $\overrightarrow{AB}$ a multiple of $\overrightarrow{AC}$ — and that test replaces any argument about gradients, which do not exist in space.
}[
    table{
        Vector                        | $\mathbf{i}$ | $\mathbf{j}$ | $\mathbf{k}$
        $\mathbf{a}$                  | 2            | -1           | 3
        $\mathbf{b}$                  | 1            | 4            | -2
        $\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}$     | 3            | 3            | 1
        $2\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{b}$    | 3            | -6           | 8
    }(
        title: Componentwise arithmetic
        header column
    )
]

scene{
    ## What does not carry over

    In the plane, two lines have two options. Either their directions are parallel, or they meet. The two lines on the canvas have different directions, so they meet somewhere — here at $(2, 2)$ — and there is no way to draw them so that they do not.

    ===

    In space there is a third option. Picture one line running along the floor and another running across the ceiling in a different direction. They are not parallel, and they never meet: there is a whole dimension of room for them to miss each other in. Lines like that are called skew, and they have no two-dimensional analogue at all.

    The consequence is that *not parallel* no longer means *intersecting*, and a question about two lines in space needs an actual test for whether they share a point. That test, and the vector form of a line it works on, are in *Vector Equations of Lines*.

    Before that comes the tool the test depends on. Everything so far has been about lengths and multiples; nothing yet measures an angle. The one product of two vectors that returns a number does exactly that, and it is set out in *The Dot Product*.

    task{
        Show that $A(1, 1, 1)$, $B(2, 3, 5)$ and $C(4, 7, 13)$ are collinear.
    }[
        $\overrightarrow{AB} = (1, 2, 4)$ and $\overrightarrow{AC} = (3, 6, 12) = 3\overrightarrow{AB}$. The two vectors are parallel and share the point $A$, so the three points lie on one line.
    ](
        title: Your turn
    )

    internote(ref: the-dot-product.chalk)
}[
    canvas.graph{
        curve(expression: 0.5 * x + 1; colour: orange; label: $L_1$)
        curve(expression: 4 - x; colour: purple; label: $L_2$)

        cue{
            point(x: 2; y: 2; colour: teal; label: $(2, 2)$)
        }(
            in: 1
        )
    }(
        x axis label: $x$
        y axis label: $y$
        x domain: [-2, 6]
        y domain: [-1, 5]
    )
]
